title: 8. Errors
nav: Learn
order: 800
template: docs
---
<p>Bayan has no exceptions: a function that can fail says so in its signature, the failure comes back as a value, and every caller must deal with it. This chapter builds an order taker that reads a quantity, takes stock from a map and reports rejected orders with an exit code, meeting each compiler error on the way.</p>
<p>The <a href="errors.html">Errors guide</a> is the short reference; this chapter shows the diagnostics. Prerequisites: maps and strings from <a href="book-07-collections.html">chapter 7</a>, unions from <a href="book-06-unions.html">chapter 6</a>.</p>

<h2 id="declare">Declaring the ways to fail</h2>
<p>An error set is a <code>union error</code>: each case is one way to fail, in camelCase, one per line, and may carry data.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    empty
    notNumber
}</code></pre>
<p>The function lists its sets after the parameters with <code>fails</code>.</p>

<h2 id="fail">Failing with <code>fail</code></h2>
<p><code>fail .empty</code> leaves the function with an error; <code>return</code> always returns a value of the return type and never fails. Both take a suffix: <code>fail .empty if text.isEmpty</code> (postfix, from <a href="reading.html">Reading Bayan code</a>) and <code>x ?? fail .notNumber</code> for a <code>null</code> optional.</p>
<example name="book-08-fail"/>
<p><code>parseQty</code> returns an <code>i64</code> or fails with <code>empty</code> or <code>notNumber</code>. The caller writes <code>catch 0</code>, the fallback when the call fails; each fallback differs on purpose, so the output tells which path ran.</p>

<h3 id="fail-mistakes">Two mistakes with <code>fail</code></h3>
<p>The case must be in a set the function lists: the leading dot looks it up in the <code>fails</code> clause.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 parseQty(string text) fails ParseError {
    fail .empty if text.isEmpty
    fail .negative if text.length &gt; 3
    return i64.tryParse(text) ?? fail .notNumber
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2603:</span> `.negative` is not in the `fails` clause of `parseQty`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> f2.byn:8:10

<span class="gut">  8 │</span>     fail .negative if text.length &gt; 3
<span class="gut">    │</span>          <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> name a case of `ParseError`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2603</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>A function with no <code>fails</code> clause cannot fail at all.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 n) {
    fail .empty if n == 0
    return n * 2
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2604:</span> `twice` cannot fail, so it cannot pass on `.empty`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> g1.byn:6:10

<span class="gut">  6 │</span>     fail .empty if n == 0
<span class="gut">    │</span>          <span class="hl">^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> declare the error in a `fails` clause, or handle it here

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2604</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="handle">Handling a failure with <code>catch</code></h2>
<p>A call to a failing function cannot ignore the possibility.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let n = parseQty("12")
    print($"{n}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `parseQty` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> f1.byn:12:13

<span class="gut">  12 │</span>     let n = parseQty("12")
<span class="gut">     │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
<span class="gut">     │</span> 12 │     let n = try parseQty("12")
<span class="gut">     │    │</span>             <span class="add">++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2602</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>The help line names both options; <code>catch</code> handles it here, in three forms: a fallback value (<code>f() catch 0</code>), a block (<code>f() catch { … }</code>), or a <code>switch</code> on the error (<code>f() catch switch { … }</code>, below).</p>
<p>When <code>f()</code> returns a value, a <code>catch</code> block has none to give back, so it must leave with <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or a panic; the compiler stops a block that falls through.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let n = parseQty("") catch {
        print("bad")
    }
    print($"{n}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2611:</span> this `catch` block can complete normally; end it with `return`, `break`, `continue`
or a panic
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> f5.byn:14:5

<span class="gut">  14 │</span>     }
<span class="gut">     │</span>     <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> leave the block instead of falling through; `parseQty` returns a value

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2611</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>A <code>void</code> call can use a block that ends normally.</p>

<h2 id="try">Passing a failure up with <code>try</code></h2>
<p>When the current function can do nothing useful, <code>try</code> passes the failure to its caller, so the function lists the error in its own <code>fails</code> clause.</p>
<example name="book-08-try"/>
<p>In <code>lineTotal</code>, <code>try parseQty(qty)</code> may pass on a <code>ParseError</code>, hence its <code>fails</code> clause. In <code>main</code> the first call succeeds; the second fails with <code>negative</code>, the <code>catch</code> block prints a message and returns, and the last <code>print</code> never runs.</p>
<p>Without a <code>fails</code> clause, an error passed with <code>try</code> has nowhere to go:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(string text) {
    return try parseQty(text)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2604:</span> `twice` cannot fail, so it cannot pass on `ParseError`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> h2.byn:11:12

<span class="gut">  11 │</span>     return try parseQty(text) 
<span class="gut">     │</span>            <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> handle the error with `catch`, or declare `fails ParseError`

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2604</span></pre>
</figure>

<h3 id="try-reach"><code>try</code> covers everything to its right</h3>
<p><code>try</code> covers the whole expression after it, down to the weakest operator: in <code>try parseQty(text) * 2</code> the compiler sees a multiplication that cannot fail. Parentheses limit it to the call.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(string text) fails ParseError {
    return try parseQty(text) * 2
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1054:</span> `try` covers `parseQty(text) * 2`, which cannot fail
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> f6.byn:12:16

<span class="gut">  12 │</span>     return try parseQty(text) * 2
<span class="gut">     │</span>                <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ cannot fail</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> wrap the part that fails in parentheses: `(try parseQty(text))`
<span class="gut">     │</span> 12 │     return (try parseQty(text)) * 2
<span class="gut">     │    │</span>            <span class="add">+++++++++++++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1054</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="switch">Choosing by error with <code>catch switch</code></h2>
<p>Switch on the error when each failure needs its own response; a case may carry data, named in the declaration and bound in the arm with <code>let</code>.</p>
<example name="book-08-switch"/>
<p>Every arm is <code>return …</code>, leaving <code>describe</code> at once; only a success reaches the last line, so <code>n</code> is the quantity. <code>.notNumber(let t)</code> binds the payload to <code>t</code>. An arm can also be an expression of the success type: <code>.empty =&gt; 0</code>.</p>
<p>The switch must cover every case; leave one out and the compiler names it.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = parseQty("12") catch switch {
    .empty =&gt; 0,
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2410:</span> `switch` does not handle every `ParseError`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> f4.byn:12:34

<span class="gut">  12 │</span>     let n = parseQty("12") catch switch {
<span class="gut">     │</span>                                  <span class="hl">^^^^^^ not covered: .notNumber</span>
<span class="gut">     │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add the missing arms, or `_ =&gt; …` for the rest

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2410</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="compose">Combining and narrowing error sets</h2>
<p>A function calling two others may fail both ways: build a bigger set from the smaller ones. The result is flat, so <code>OrderError</code> has the cases of both, and a <code>ParseError</code> converts into it.</p>
<example name="book-08-compose"/>
<p>In four parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>union error OrderError = ParseError, StockError</code> combines two sets.</li>
<li><code>union error Retryable = OrderError except .notNumber</code> is the same set without one case.</li>
<li>In <code>retrying</code>, <code>catch (ParseError.notNumber) 0</code> handles that case and lets the others through, so the rest must be in <code>fails Retryable</code>, not <code>fails OrderError</code>.</li>
<li>In <code>main</code>, <code>ParseError e</code> matches every case of that set, bound as <code>e</code>, while <code>.outOfStock(let item)</code> matches one. The output is <code>-2</code>: <code>take</code> asked for 9 and had 3.</li>
</ul>
<p>Shared case names block combination: a flat set cannot have two cases with one name.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    empty
}

union error Other {
    empty
}

union error Both = ParseError, Other</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3202:</span> `ParseError` and `Other` both have a case `empty`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> h3.byn:9:1

<span class="gut">  9 │</span> union error Both = ParseError, Other
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^ two cases `empty`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> rename one case, or compose fewer sets

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN3202</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>Cases are namespaced by set (<code>ParseError.empty</code> is not <code>Other.empty</code>); a bare <code>.empty</code> matching two sets is an error.</p>

<h3 id="public-fails">Who has to write the list</h3>
<p>A public function must list its errors, so anyone outside the package reads them from the signature.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public i64 parseQty(string text) fails {
    return 1
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2607:</span> public function `parseQty` must list its errors
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> h4.byn:1:34

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> public i64 parseQty(string text) fails {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                  <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2607</span></pre>
</figure>
<p>A private function may write a bare <code>fails</code> and let the compiler infer the set; inside it, name the set when failing (<code>fail ParseError.empty</code>), since the dot has no clause. The editor hints the inferred set.</p>

<h2 id="main">Errors in <code>main</code></h2>
<p><code>main</code> may fail: the process prints the error's doc comment (<code>///</code>) to standard error and exits with code 1, or the <code>@exitCode(n)</code> code on the case. Deferred statements run before the exit.</p>
<example name="book-08-main"/>
<p>Standard output shows the four orders then <code>shift over</code>, printed by <code>defer</code> on the way out; <code>error: Some orders were rejected.</code> goes to standard error. Run <code>echo $?</code> to see exit code 3.</p>
<p>The quantity inside <code>rejected(i64 count)</code> is not printed, only the doc comment; catch the error in a caller to use the data.</p>

<h2 id="panic">Panics are not errors</h2>
<p>A panic is a bug no caller can fix: an index out of range, a missing map key, division by zero, integer overflow. It prints a message and the line, then stops the program.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>panic: division by zero
  at h1.byn:3</pre>
</figure>
<p>Integer arithmetic checks for overflow too:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>panic: arithmetic overflow
  at g4.byn:3</pre>
</figure>
<p>Panics are not listed in <code>fails</code> and cannot be <code>catch</code>ed, and plain <code>defer</code> does not run during one. Use an error for <em>good</em> code with bad input, like a quantity typed as <code>abc</code>; a panic is only for a bug, as is a broken <code>expects</code> precondition, which panics rather than fails.</p>

<h2 id="planned">What is planned</h2>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. The specification has <code>except</code> on a sequence (below), error return traces in debug builds and <code>@status(n)</code> for web responses; the compiler rejects <code>except</code>.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">for line in lines except "" {
    print(line)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2022:</span> this expression is not supported in v0.1
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> i2.byn:3:14

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     for w in words except null {
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2022</span></pre>
</figure>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>What does <code>book-08-fail</code> print for the input <code>"twelve"</code>?</summary>
<p><code>-2</code> is the third <code>catch</code> fallback: <code>i64.tryParse("twelve")</code> is <code>null</code>, so <code>?? fail .notNumber</code> fails; <code>12</code> answers <code>"12"</code> and <code>-1</code> the empty string.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>A function calls <code>try parse(text)</code> but has no <code>fails</code> clause. What happens?</summary>
<p>It does not compile (BYN2604): the function cannot pass the error on because it says it cannot fail. Add a <code>fails</code> clause or <code>catch</code> it; it does not panic at run time, and no public <code>fails</code> is inferred.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>In <code>book-08-try</code>, the call <code>lineTotal("-3", by: 250) catch { … }</code> prints a message and then <code>return</code>s. Why does the program not print a total after it?</summary>
<p>The block ends with <code>return</code>, which leaves <code>main</code>; the last <code>print</code> is after it. A block that fell through would be refused: no value for <code>bad</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>What exit code does <code>book-08-main</code> give, and where does the text <code>Some orders were rejected.</code> go?</summary>
<p>Exit code 3, from <code>@exitCode(3)</code>; without it the code would be 1. The text is the case's doc comment, on standard error, not standard output.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>You index a list with <code>xs[10]</code> and the list has 2 elements. Which of these can you write to recover? (a) <code>xs[10] catch 0</code> (b) <code>try xs[10]</code> (c) neither</summary>
<p>(c). An out-of-range index is a panic, not an error: no <code>fails</code> clause to <code>catch</code> or <code>try</code>. Ask a question that can come back empty: <code>xs.tryGet(at: 10) ?? 0</code>.</p></details>

